---
// The Companies tab in English: the one body written here and not in
// the README, so the standard on GitHub and in llms.txt carries no
// commercial part. Each language has its own copy of this section
// (prose, not a dictionary of strings); a missing one falls back to it.
import { contact } from './contact';
---
  <section id="companies" class="co" aria-label="Companies">
    <p class="co-over">For companies</p>
    <h2 class="co-title">Every use of AI in the company, with a person in charge of it.</h2>
    <p class="co-lede">Your teams already work with AI: in code, in reports, in processes
      that run on their own. What a customer, an auditor or your own board will ask is
      not whether AI was used. It is who decided it should be, who understands what it
      does, and who answers when it fails. <span class="wm">talaia</span> gives the company
      that answer: values held across the organization, a way of working that keeps
      judgment with people, and a signature that shows it in every piece of work.</p>

    <h3>What the company adopts</h3>
    <p>Not a tool, and not a new process. A position on AI, held by the whole
      organization before any tool is chosen.</p>
    <ul class="co-rows">
      <li><strong>The manifesto</strong> The company's values on AI: human judgment over
        machine autonomy, a person who answers over a system with no owner,
        understanding what goes out over accepting what works, and knowing when not to
        use it.</li>
      <li><strong>Human-in-charge</strong> Every use of AI has a named person in charge:
        someone who decided it, designed it, understands it and answers for it. That
        includes the automations nobody watches: the person answers for the system, not
        for each thing it produces.</li>
      <li><strong>The framework</strong> The line between what may be delegated and what
        must stay human. The design written once per system, then Brief, Build, Watch,
        Sign for each piece of work, under the process you already have.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>One practice, three directions</h3>
    <p>The Certificate of Understanding is how all of it shows: the same two clauses a
      single person signs. Adopted by a company, it becomes a good practice that travels
      with the work.</p>
    <ul class="co-rows">
      <li><strong>Inside the company</strong> Every piece of work done with AI (code, a
        report, a post, an automation) carries the name of a person who understands it
        and answers for it. The names accumulate as the work goes up: the author, the
        reviewer, the board.</li>
      <li><strong>To your customers</strong> Deliver the certificate as a seal on what
        you hand over: an AI helped make this, and a named person stands behind it.</li>
      <li><strong>From your distributors</strong> Require it of those whose work you
        receive, the way you already require any other standard of quality.</li>
    </ul>

    <section class="co-offer">
      <p class="co-over">Services</p>
      <h3>We help you adopt it, or we build it for you.</h3>

      <h4 class="co-group">Adopting <span class="wm">talaia</span></h4>
      <ul class="co-grid co-3">
        <li><strong class="co-name">Training</strong>
          <span class="co-hook">Teams that get more from AI, and know where it stops.</span>
          <span>For every role, not only engineers.</span></li>
        <li><strong class="co-name">Audit</strong>
          <span class="co-hook">Know where you stand before anyone asks.</span>
          <span>Every use of AI mapped: who owns it, what is signed, what is not.</span></li>
        <li><strong class="co-name">Consulting</strong>
          <span class="co-hook"><span class="wm">talaia</span>, fitted to how you already work.</span>
          <span>Your processes, your tools. Nothing thrown away.</span></li>
      </ul>

      <h4 class="co-group">Engineering</h4>
      <ul class="co-grid">
        <li><strong class="co-name">AI projects</strong>
          <span class="co-hook">Agents and automations that take real work off your plate.</span>
          <span>And an honest no when AI is not the answer.</span></li>
        <li><strong class="co-name">Software engineering</strong>
          <span class="co-hook">Products built fast, owned by your team.</span>
          <span>Designed by people, built inside written rules.</span></li>
        <li><strong class="co-name">Process optimization</strong>
          <span class="co-hook">Find where the time goes.</span>
          <span>Automate what should be, with a person in charge of every step.</span></li>
        <li><strong class="co-name">Forward deployed engineers</strong>
          <span class="co-hook">Our engineers, inside your team.</span>
          <span>Delivering with your people until it runs without us.</span></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="co-offer">
      <p class="co-over">Products</p>
      <h3>Proof you can show. Software that builds itself.</h3>

      <div class="co-product">
        <div>
          <strong class="co-name">Official certification</strong>
          <p class="co-hook">Proof, not promises.</p>
          <p>Anyone can adopt the open standard on their own word. Official certification
            puts our name behind yours: a seal your customers can check, in every tender
            and every contract.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn" href="#contact">Get certified</a>
      </div>

      <div class="co-feature">
        <p class="co-over"><span class="wm">aguait</span></p>
        <h3>Software that builds itself.<br />People who answer for it.</h3>
        <p class="co-kicker"><span class="wm">aguait</span> is automatic software development on the principles of <span class="wm">talaia</span>.
          We set it up in your company.</p>
        <ul class="co-points">
          <li><strong>Your design</strong> You write the design once. <span class="wm">aguait</span> builds from it.</li>
          <li><strong>On its own</strong> From design to finished change, no code typed.</li>
          <li><strong>Signed</strong> Nothing ships without a name behind it.</li>
          <li><strong>With you</strong> We install it, tune it and train who runs it.</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#contact"><span>Get <span class="wm">aguait</span> for your company</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="co-cta" id="contact">
      <h3>Bring <span class="wm">talaia</span> to your company</h3>
      <p>Tell us where you are. We will show you where to start.</p>
      <form class="co-form" action={contact} method="POST">
        <label>Name <input name="name" autocomplete="name" /></label>
        <label>Company <input name="company" autocomplete="organization" /></label>
        <label>Email <input type="email" name="email" autocomplete="email" required /></label>
        <label>Service
          <select name="service">
            <option value="">Not sure yet</option>
            <optgroup label="Products">
              <option>Official certification</option>
              <option>aguait</option>
            </optgroup>
            <optgroup label="Services: adopting talaia">
              <option>Training</option>
              <option>Audit</option>
              <option>Consulting</option>
            </optgroup>
            <optgroup label="Services: engineering">
              <option>AI projects</option>
              <option>Software engineering</option>
              <option>Process optimization</option>
              <option>Forward deployed engineers</option>
            </optgroup>
          </select>
        </label>
        <label class="wide">Message <textarea name="message" rows="5" required></textarea></label>
        <div class="co-send">
          <button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit">Talk to us</button>
          <p class="co-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
      </form>
    </div>
    <script is:inline>
      // Sends the form without leaving the page. Without JS the form
      // posts to Formspree as it is, and Formspree's own page answers.
      (() => {
        const form = document.querySelector('.co-form');
        const status = form.querySelector('.co-status');
        const button = form.querySelector('button');
        form.addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
          e.preventDefault();
          button.disabled = true;
          status.textContent = 'Sending…';
          try {
            const res = await fetch(form.action, {
              method: 'POST', body: new FormData(form), headers: { Accept: 'application/json' },
            });
            if (!res.ok) throw new Error(res.statusText);
            form.reset();
            status.textContent = 'Thank you, your message has been sent.';
          } catch {
            status.textContent = 'The message could not be sent. Please try again.';
          } finally {
            button.disabled = false;
          }
        });
      })();
    </script>
  </section>
